{"preference":"B","margin":5,"reasons":"No separate reference material was supplied, so I checked each candidate against the other and against its own internal arithmetic. B's figures are internally consistent: 2.9 million divided by 4,218 is about 688. B also quotes the evaluator with the caveat intact. A rounds the enrollment up to 'nearly 5,000', which does not match the 688 per-student cost it states. A drops the non-randomized caveat, claims the results prove the model works, and its Okafor quote reads as altered and much stronger than B's version.","objections":[{"candidate":"A","category":"accuracy","quote":"nearly 5,000 students in grades 1 through 3","claim":"Enrollment is given as nearly 5,000, but B gives 4,218, and $2.9M / 4,218 = $688. At 5,000 students the per-student cost would be about $580, so A's own figures conflict.","severity":"major"},{"candidate":"A","category":"source_support","quote":"a 38 percent advantage that proves the model works","claim":"The 38 percent figure is a ratio of the gains (3/8), not a stated finding. 'Proves' is far stronger than a non-randomized internal pilot comparison supports.","severity":"critical"},{"candidate":"A","category":"accuracy","quote":"\"the results are encouraging and show that tutoring works.\"","claim":"B attributes to the evaluator a statement that results 'do not yet show that tutoring caused the gains'. A's quote reverses the evaluator's caution into endorsement.","severity":"critical"},{"candidate":"A","category":"completeness","quote":"The effect was unmistakable.","claim":"A omits that the comparison was an internal pilot comparison and not randomized, and that selection effects may explain the difference. This is material to a budget vote.","severity":"critical"},{"candidate":"A","category":"source_support","quote":"A randomized study planned for 2026-27 will confirm what the first year has already made clear.","claim":"The study is presented as confirming a conclusion in advance. The evaluator said causation is not yet shown.","severity":"major"},{"candidate":"A","category":"source_support","quote":"the Corps delivered results that larger programs have struggled to match","claim":"This comparison to larger programs has no support in either candidate.","severity":"major"},{"candidate":"A","category":"completeness","quote":"sat down with a tutor three times a week","claim":"The 30-minute session length that B includes is missing.","severity":"minor"}],"allegations":[{"candidate":"A","quote":"nearly 5,000 students","claim":"The enrollment figure appears to differ from the 4,218 students in the other candidate and does not fit the stated $688 per student."},{"candidate":"A","quote":"\"the results are encouraging and show that tutoring works.\"","claim":"The evaluator's wording appears to have been altered. The other candidate quotes it as 'encouraging but do not yet show that tutoring caused the gains'."},{"candidate":"A","quote":"larger programs have struggled to match","claim":"No source supports the comparison to larger programs."}],"out_of_lane_notes":"B is much more usable for a board member: it gives a short version up front and a concrete instruction on how to describe the results. A's title and tone are promotional, which does not suit the stated purpose."}